---
// The Docker install's steps. Shown on their own until a Windows installer is published, then under "Mac, Linux, or Docker".
---
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Install Docker Desktop.</strong> <a href="https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop">Get it from Docker</a> (free for personal and educational use) and make sure it is running. This is the longest step.</li>
  <li><strong>Download and unzip</strong> the MyDegreePlan folder anywhere, for example in Documents.</li>
  <li><strong>Start it.</strong> On Windows, double-click <code>mdp.cmd</code>. On Mac or Linux, open a terminal in the folder and run <code>sh mdp.sh start</code>. The first start takes a few minutes, then your browser opens. If Windows says "Windows protected your PC", choose <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p class="small">Create an account with any email and an 8+ character password. Nothing is emailed or verified. Updates then arrive through an <strong>Update now</strong> button inside the app.</p>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:20px">
  <h3>Coming back later</h3>
  <p>On Windows, the first start adds a <strong>MyDegreePlan</strong> shortcut to your Desktop. Double-click it and it starts Docker Desktop if needed, then opens the app. You can also bookmark <code>http://localhost:8080</code>: once Docker Desktop is running, the app is already there. Keep the <code>.env</code> file in the folder, because it belongs to your data.</p>
</div>
